933	   * Preconditions.checkNotNull is *intended* for performing eager null checks on parameters that a
934	   * nullness checker can already "prove" are non-null. That means that the first parameter to
935	   * checkNotNull *should* be annotated to require it to be non-null.
936	   *
937	   * However, for a variety of reasons, Google developers have written a ton of code over the past
938	   * decade that assumes that they can use checkNotNull for non-precondition checks. I had hoped to
939	   * take a principled stand on this, but the amount of such code is simply overwhelming. To avoid
940	   * creating a lot of compile errors that users would not find to be informative, we're giving in
941	   * and allowing callers to pass arguments that a nullness checker believes could be null.
942	   *
943	   * We still encourage people to use requireNonNull over checkNotNull for non-precondition checks.
