45	/**
46	 * The {@code CycleDetectingLockFactory} creates {@link ReentrantLock} instances and {@link
47	 * ReentrantReadWriteLock} instances that detect potential deadlock by checking for cycles in lock
48	 * acquisition order.
49	 *
50	 * <p>Potential deadlocks detected when calling the {@code lock()}, {@code lockInterruptibly()}, or
51	 * {@code tryLock()} methods will result in the execution of the {@link Policy} specified when
52	 * creating the factory. The currently available policies are:
53	 *
54	 * <ul>
55	 *   <li>DISABLED
56	 *   <li>WARN
57	 *   <li>THROW
58	 * </ul>
59	 *
60	 * <p>The locks created by a factory instance will detect lock acquisition cycles with locks created
61	 * by other {@code CycleDetectingLockFactory} instances (except those with {@code Policy.DISABLED}).
62	 * A lock's behavior when a cycle is detected, however, is defined by the {@code Policy} of the
63	 * factory that created it. This allows detection of cycles across components while delegating
64	 * control over lock behavior to individual components.
65	 *
66	 * <p>Applications are encouraged to use a {@code CycleDetectingLockFactory} to create any locks for
67	 * which external/unmanaged code is executed while the lock is held. (See caveats under
68	 * <strong>Performance</strong>).
69	 *
70	 * <p><strong>Cycle Detection</strong>
71	 *
72	 * <p>Deadlocks can arise when locks are acquired in an order that forms a cycle. In a simple
73	 * example involving two locks and two threads, deadlock occurs when one thread acquires Lock A, and
74	 * then Lock B, while another thread acquires Lock B, and then Lock A:
75	 *
76	 * <pre>
77	 * Thread1: acquire(LockA) --X acquire(LockB)
78	 * Thread2: acquire(LockB) --X acquire(LockA)
79	 * </pre>
80	 *
81	 * <p>Neither thread will progress because each is waiting for the other. In more complex
82	 * applications, cycles can arise from interactions among more than 2 locks:
83	 *
84	 * <pre>
85	 * Thread1: acquire(LockA) --X acquire(LockB)
86	 * Thread2: acquire(LockB) --X acquire(LockC)
87	 * ...
88	 * ThreadN: acquire(LockN) --X acquire(LockA)
89	 * </pre>
90	 *
91	 * <p>The implementation detects cycles by constructing a directed graph in which each lock
92	 * represents a node and each edge represents an acquisition ordering between two locks.
93	 *
94	 * <ul>
95	 *   <li>Each lock adds (and removes) itself to/from a ThreadLocal Set of acquired locks when the
96	 *       Thread acquires its first hold (and releases its last remaining hold).
97	 *   <li>Before the lock is acquired, the lock is checked against the current set of acquired
98	 *       locks---to each of the acquired locks, an edge from the soon-to-be-acquired lock is either
99	 *       verified or created.
100	 *   <li>If a new edge needs to be created, the outgoing edges of the acquired locks are traversed
101	 *       to check for a cycle that reaches the lock to be acquired. If no cycle is detected, a new
102	 *       "safe" edge is created.
103	 *   <li>If a cycle is detected, an "unsafe" (cyclic) edge is created to represent a potential
104	 *       deadlock situation, and the appropriate Policy is executed.
105	 * </ul>
106	 *
107	 * <p>Note that detection of potential deadlock does not necessarily indicate that deadlock will
108	 * happen, as it is possible that higher level application logic prevents the cyclic lock
109	 * acquisition from occurring. One example of a false positive is:
110	 *
111	 * <pre>
112	 * LockA -&gt; LockB -&gt; LockC
113	 * LockA -&gt; LockC -&gt; LockB
114	 * </pre>
115	 *
116	 * <p><strong>ReadWriteLocks</strong>
117	 *
118	 * <p>While {@code ReadWriteLock} instances have different properties and can form cycles without
119	 * potential deadlock, this class treats {@code ReadWriteLock} instances as equivalent to
120	 * traditional exclusive locks. Although this increases the false positives that the locks detect
121	 * (i.e. cycles that will not actually result in deadlock), it simplifies the algorithm and
122	 * implementation considerably. The assumption is that a user of this factory wishes to eliminate
123	 * any cyclic acquisition ordering.
124	 *
125	 * <p><strong>Explicit Lock Acquisition Ordering</strong>
126	 *
127	 * <p>The {@link CycleDetectingLockFactory.WithExplicitOrdering} class can be used to enforce an
128	 * application-specific ordering in addition to performing general cycle detection.
129	 *
130	 * <p><strong>Garbage Collection</strong>
131	 *
132	 * <p>In order to allow proper garbage collection of unused locks, the edges of the lock graph are
133	 * weak references.
134	 *
135	 * <p><strong>Performance</strong>
136	 *
137	 * <p>The extra bookkeeping done by cycle detecting locks comes at some cost to performance.
138	 * Benchmarks (as of December 2011) show that:
139	 *
140	 * <ul>
141	 *   <li>for an unnested {@code lock()} and {@code unlock()}, a cycle detecting lock takes 38ns as
142	 *       opposed to the 24ns taken by a plain lock.
143	 *   <li>for nested locking, the cost increases with the depth of the nesting:
144	 *       <ul>
145	 *         <li>2 levels: average of 64ns per lock()/unlock()
146	 *         <li>3 levels: average of 77ns per lock()/unlock()
147	 *         <li>4 levels: average of 99ns per lock()/unlock()
148	 *         <li>5 levels: average of 103ns per lock()/unlock()
149	 *         <li>10 levels: average of 184ns per lock()/unlock()
150	 *         <li>20 levels: average of 393ns per lock()/unlock()
151	 *       </ul>
152	 * </ul>
153	 *
154	 * <p>As such, the CycleDetectingLockFactory may not be suitable for performance-critical
155	 * applications which involve tightly-looped or deeply-nested locking algorithms.
156	 *
157	 * @author Darick Tong
158	 * @since 13.0
159	 */
160	@J2ktIncompatible
161	@GwtIncompatible
162	public class CycleDetectingLockFactory {
163	
164	  /**
165	   * Encapsulates the action to be taken when a potential deadlock is encountered. Clients can use
166	   * one of the predefined {@link Policies} or specify a custom implementation. Implementations must
167	   * be thread-safe.
168	   *
169	   * @since 13.0
170	   */
171	  public interface Policy {
172	
173	    /**
174	     * Called when a potential deadlock is encountered. Implementations can throw the given {@code
175	     * exception} and/or execute other desired logic.
176	     *
177	     * <p>Note that the method will be called even upon an invocation of {@code tryLock()}. Although
178	     * {@code tryLock()} technically recovers from deadlock by eventually timing out, this behavior
179	     * is chosen based on the assumption that it is the application's wish to prohibit any cyclical
180	     * lock acquisitions.
181	     */
182	    void handlePotentialDeadlock(PotentialDeadlockException exception);
183	  }
184	
185	  /**
186	   * Pre-defined {@link Policy} implementations.
187	   *
188	   * @since 13.0
189	   */
190	  public enum Policies implements Policy {
191	    /**
192	     * When potential deadlock is detected, this policy results in the throwing of the {@code
193	     * PotentialDeadlockException} indicating the potential deadlock, which includes stack traces
194	     * illustrating the cycle in lock acquisition order.
195	     */
196	    THROW {
197	      @Override
198	      public void handlePotentialDeadlock(PotentialDeadlockException e) {
199	        throw e;
200	      }
201	    },
202	
203	    /**
204	     * When potential deadlock is detected, this policy results in the logging of a {@link
205	     * Level#SEVERE} message indicating the potential deadlock, which includes stack traces
206	     * illustrating the cycle in lock acquisition order.
207	     */
208	    WARN {
209	      @Override
210	      public void handlePotentialDeadlock(PotentialDeadlockException e) {
211	        logger.get().log(Level.SEVERE, "Detected potential deadlock", e);
212	      }
213	    },
214	
215	    /**
216	     * Disables cycle detection. This option causes the factory to return unmodified lock
217	     * implementations provided by the JDK, and is provided to allow applications to easily
218	     * parameterize when cycle detection is enabled.
219	     *
220	     * <p>Note that locks created by a factory with this policy will <em>not</em> participate the
221	     * cycle detection performed by locks created by other factories.
222	     */
223	    DISABLED {
224	      @Override
225	      public void handlePotentialDeadlock(PotentialDeadlockException e) {}
226	    };
227	  }
228	
229	  /** Creates a new factory with the specified policy. */
230	  public static CycleDetectingLockFactory newInstance(Policy policy) {
231	    return new CycleDetectingLockFactory(policy);
232	  }
233	
234	  /** Equivalent to {@code newReentrantLock(lockName, false)}. */
235	  public ReentrantLock newReentrantLock(String lockName) {
236	    return newReentrantLock(lockName, false);
237	  }
238	
239	  /**
240	   * Creates a {@link ReentrantLock} with the given fairness policy. The {@code lockName} is used in
241	   * the warning or exception output to help identify the locks involved in the detected deadlock.
242	   */
243	  public ReentrantLock newReentrantLock(String lockName, boolean fair) {
244	    return policy == Policies.DISABLED
245	        ? new ReentrantLock(fair)
246	        : new CycleDetectingReentrantLock(new LockGraphNode(lockName), fair);
247	  }
248	
249	  /** Equivalent to {@code newReentrantReadWriteLock(lockName, false)}. */
250	  public ReentrantReadWriteLock newReentrantReadWriteLock(String lockName) {
251	    return newReentrantReadWriteLock(lockName, false);
252	  }
253	
254	  /**
255	   * Creates a {@link ReentrantReadWriteLock} with the given fairness policy. The {@code lockName}
256	   * is used in the warning or exception output to help identify the locks involved in the detected
257	   * deadlock.
258	   */
259	  public ReentrantReadWriteLock newReentrantReadWriteLock(String lockName, boolean fair) {
260	    return policy == Policies.DISABLED
261	        ? new ReentrantReadWriteLock(fair)
262	        : new CycleDetectingReentrantReadWriteLock(new LockGraphNode(lockName), fair);
263	  }
264	
265	  // A static mapping from an Enum type to its set of LockGraphNodes.
266	  private static final ConcurrentMap<
267	          Class<? extends Enum<?>>, Map<? extends Enum<?>, LockGraphNode>>
268	      lockGraphNodesPerType = new MapMaker().weakKeys().makeMap();
269	
270	  /** Creates a {@code CycleDetectingLockFactory.WithExplicitOrdering<E>}. */
271	  public static <E extends Enum<E>> WithExplicitOrdering<E> newInstanceWithExplicitOrdering(
272	      Class<E> enumClass, Policy policy) {
273	    // createNodes maps each enumClass to a Map with the corresponding enum key
274	    // type.
275	    checkNotNull(enumClass);
276	    checkNotNull(policy);
277	    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
278	    Map<E, LockGraphNode> lockGraphNodes = (Map<E, LockGraphNode>) getOrCreateNodes(enumClass);
279	    return new WithExplicitOrdering<>(policy, lockGraphNodes);
280	  }
281	
282	  @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
283	  private static <E extends Enum<E>> Map<? extends E, LockGraphNode> getOrCreateNodes(
284	      Class<E> clazz) {
285	    Map<E, LockGraphNode> existing = (Map<E, LockGraphNode>) lockGraphNodesPerType.get(clazz);
286	    if (existing != null) {
287	      return existing;
288	    }
289	    Map<E, LockGraphNode> created = createNodes(clazz);
290	    existing = (Map<E, LockGraphNode>) lockGraphNodesPerType.putIfAbsent(clazz, created);
291	    return MoreObjects.firstNonNull(existing, created);
292	  }
293	
294	  /**
295	   * For a given Enum type, creates an immutable map from each of the Enum's values to a
296	   * corresponding LockGraphNode, with the {@code allowedPriorLocks} and {@code
297	   * disallowedPriorLocks} prepopulated with nodes according to the natural ordering of the
298	   * associated Enum values.
299	   */
300	  @VisibleForTesting
301	  static <E extends Enum<E>> Map<E, LockGraphNode> createNodes(Class<E> clazz) {
302	    EnumMap<E, LockGraphNode> map = new EnumMap<>(clazz);
303	    E[] keys = clazz.getEnumConstants();
304	    int numKeys = keys.length;
305	    ArrayList<LockGraphNode> nodes = newArrayListWithCapacity(numKeys);
306	    // Create a LockGraphNode for each enum value.
307	    for (E key : keys) {
308	      LockGraphNode node = new LockGraphNode(getLockName(key));
309	      nodes.add(node);
310	      map.put(key, node);
311	    }
312	    // Pre-populate all allowedPriorLocks with nodes of smaller ordinal.
313	    for (int i = 1; i < numKeys; i++) {
314	      nodes.get(i).checkAcquiredLocks(Policies.THROW, nodes.subList(0, i));
315	    }
316	    // Pre-populate all disallowedPriorLocks with nodes of larger ordinal.
317	    for (int i = 0; i < numKeys - 1; i++) {
318	      nodes.get(i).checkAcquiredLocks(Policies.DISABLED, nodes.subList(i + 1, numKeys));
319	    }
320	    return unmodifiableMap(map);
321	  }
